[Role "teaching"]

{ Today we learn how to finish a game. First the back rank mate. His own pawns block every way off his back row. [%csl Rg8] } 37... Rxd1 { He grabs your rook. Now march your other rook all the way to his back row. [%cal Yc1c8] } 38. Rc8+ { His king is walled in by his own pawns. He has to block. [%csl Rf7,Rg7,Rh7] } (38. Rxd1 { You take back. But the rook was not the prize here, the mate was. } 38... Re2+ 39. Kc1 g6 { The window is open, the mate is gone, and you are still losing. [%csl Gg6] }) (38. Rc7 { The seventh rank looks strong. But look at his knight. } 38... Nxc7 39. Kc2 Rg1 { The knight ate your rook for free. [%csl Rc7] }) 38... Rd8 { He blocks. Take the blocker, the rank is what matters. [%cal Yc8d8] } 39. Rxd8+ { Good. He still has one rook to throw in front. [%csl Re3] } (39. Rc1 { You back off, and he takes over the first rank. } 39... Rd2+ 40. Rc2 Ree2 { Now it is your own king in the net. [%csl Rb2] }) 39... Re8 { The last blocker. Take it and nothing is left. [%cal Yd8e8] } 40. Rxe8# { Mate! His own pawns kept him at home — that is the back rank mate. [%csl Rg8,Gf7,Gg7,Gh7] } (40. Rd7 { You had mate in one. This only pokes at pawns. } 40... Rb8 41. Rd5 Nc7 { The mate has gone and now he is the one winning. }) (40. Rd1 { A quiet retreat with mate sitting on the board. } 40... h6 41. Kc2 Re2+ { You handed the game straight back. }) 1-0


