{ His rook on f two is eyeing your queen on d two, and he has no idea what is coming. Mate in two. } 21... Rxd2 { He takes the queen, and thinks that settles it. } 22. Bxe6+ { The bishop gives check and there is only one square for him. } (22. Rd1 { You chase the rook away. That rook was never the problem. } 22... Rxd1 23. Rxd1 Qf2 { The mate has gone and you are simply a queen down. }) (22. Rf8+ { A rook check looks brave. Count the guards first. } 22... Kxf8 23. c5 Qf2 24. Bxe6 Qxe3 { He is simply a rook up and safe. }) (22. Bf3 { You save the bishop and lose the game. } 22... exf3 23. Rf2 Rxf2 24. Kh2 Qg5 { His pawn and rook do the mating now. }) 22... Kh8 { Into the corner, next to his own pawns. Now deliver the mate. } 23. Rf8# { Mate! The rook lands on the empty back rank. [%csl Rh8,Gg7,Gh7] } (23. Rf7 { You go pawn hunting and forget the mate. } 23... h5 24. g3 Qg5 25. Rf5 Qxf5 { Now he is better and your attack is over. }) (23. Bg8 { A cute idea, but he just eats it. } 23... Kxg8 24. Rf4 Qg3 25. Rg4 Qxe3 { A piece down, and no mate anywhere. }) (23. Rf4 { Chasing the queen with mate on the board. } 23... Qe7 24. d5 g5 25. Rf5 Bc6 { The mate has gone for good. }) 1-0


