{ His queen and yours can already see each other across the board. Keep an eye on his back rank too. } 16. Qxb4 { He takes your queen, confident the game is over. } Rd1+ { You go for the throat instead. Everything he throws in front just feeds you. } (16... Bxb4 { You win the queen back and the game is level. The mate was worth more. } 17. Rd3 b5 18. Nc3 Bc5 { A normal game, and you had a forced mate. }) (16... O-O { You castle to safety, but the game was on the other side. } 17. Qxc5 Rd1+ 18. Qg1 Rxg1+ 19. Kxg1 { A piece down for nothing. }) (16... Rd4 { It looks active, and it gives him a free move. } 17. Qxc5 Rd1+ 18. Qg1 Rxg1+ 19. Kxg1 O-O { He is a piece up and perfectly safe. }) 17. Qe1 { He blocks the check by bringing the queen straight back to e one. } Rxe1+ { Take the queen, and keep the pressure on. } (17... Rxc1 { You grab the bishop and the mate walks away. } 18. Rxc1 b6 19. Qd2 f5 { A queen down, and now you are just lost. }) (17... Rd8 { You retreat with mate in your hands. } 18. Nxc5 O-O 19. Nd3 { Nothing left but a bad game. }) 18. Rf1 { He blocks again, this time with his other rook. } Rxf1# { Mate! His king never had a square to run to. [%csl Rh1,Gg2,Gh2] } (18... Bb4 { A good square, but you had mate in one. } 19. Rxe1 Bxe1 20. Be3 b5 { You gave away a whole win. }) (18... Bf2 { Same story: mate was there and you played a quiet move. } 19. Rxe1 Bxe1 20. Be3 Nc8 { The win has gone. }) 0-1


