{ Last one: the queen kiss. She lands right beside his king, guarded by a friend so he cannot just take her. } 26... Qc6 { He moves his queen to c six, out of the way. Now bring your queen in beside his king — take on f seven. [%cal Yf5f7][%csl Gd7] } 27. Qxf7+ { Your rook on d seven guards her, so he cannot take. [%csl Gd7] } (27. Rxf7 { The rook takes and the whole idea disappears. } 27... h6 28. Re7 Rf8 29. Qe5 Qf6 { Level, and the mate has gone. }) (27. Re7 { A fine looking square, but it is only a rook move. } 27... Rf8 28. Qf4 c4 29. a5 Qd5 { Equal, and his king is safe. }) (27. Qxc5 { A free pawn while mate sat on the board. } 27... Qxc5 28. Rd1 Qb4 29. Ra1 { You traded into nothing. }) (27. Rd8+ { A check that just gives the rook away. } 27... Rxd8 28. Qg4 Qf6 29. a5 { A rook down. }) 27... Kh8 { The only square he has. Now do it again, still guarded. [%cal Yf7g7] } 28. Qxg7# { Mate! The rook behind her is what makes it work. That is the queen kiss. [%csl Gd7,Rh8] } (28. Rd8+ { A check instead of the mate. } 28... Rxd8 29. Qc4 Re8 30. a5 { Mate in one, and you played this. }) (28. Ra7 { The rook wanders off and takes the mate with it. } 28... Qf6 29. Qxf6 gxf6 { A dead equal endgame. }) 1-0


