{ His king and queen huddle together on the king side. Mate in two. } 38... Kg7 { His king steps up to help — and walks straight into your net. } 39. Qh8+ { Your rook on h two guards h eight, so he cannot take her. [%csl Gh2] } (39. Qxa6 { A free pawn with mate in two on the board. } 39... Qa2+ 40. Kf1 Qb1+ 41. Kg2 Qe4+ { Now he checks you forever. }) (39. Qe4 { A strong central square, but he simply defends. } 39... Rh5 40. Rxh5 Qxh5+ 41. Kf1 Qh3+ { The mate is gone and your king is the loose one. }) (39. Qd8 { You tuck the queen away and he starts checking. } 39... Qc4+ 40. Ke3 Qc3+ 41. Ke2 Qe1+ { A draw at best now. }) 39... Kg6 { He has to walk forward. Now finish the net. } 40. Qh6# { Mate! That is the queen kiss again: the rook holds her in place, and his own rook blocks f five. [%csl Gh2,Rg6] } (40. f4 { A pawn push with mate in one available. } 40... Qg7 41. Qe8+ Kf6 42. Rh8 { The win has slipped away. }) (40. Kf1 { Your king goes for a walk while mate was there. } 40... Bd2 41. Qb8 Be3 42. Qxd6 { You lost the thread completely. }) (40. Qh4 { Very close, but that square does not mate. } 40... Qg7 41. Qe4+ Kf6 42. Kf1 { He is out. }) 1-0


